# Gate runner: --only, the container's environment, and a verdict that never printed (2026-10-04)

Two changes to the verification runners and two defects found in them while making the
changes. The runners are the instrument every other measurement in `docs/verification/` is
taken with, so a defect here is a defect in the reading of everything else:
`scripts/verify-gates.sh` runs the gate list on the host, `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` runs
it in a container, and the host runner extracts its gate list from the container one rather
than keeping a second copy.

Test names are kept on one line each, wider than the prose, because they are quoted output.

== 1. The host runner copied the container's command line but not its environment

`--with-build` makes the host runner include the build gates (cargo check, clippy, the
workspace test, the GUI protocol types). It ran the container's `cargo test` line verbatim and
reproduced a failure that had nothing to do with the tree:

$ scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
    PASS  secret scan
    PASS  cargo check
    PASS  cargo clippy
    FAIL  cargo test (exit 101)
    (the failing target's own test output, 30+ lines, elided here)
        error: 1 target failed:
            `-p xai-grok-shell --lib`
    PASS  GUI protocol types

    FAILED gates: cargo test
    34 gate(s) run, 0 skipped, 1 failed

The same command with the container's stack size set by hand is green, over the same tree, in
858 s. The status is read from `PIPESTATUS` because a pipeline's `$?` would be `tee`'s, which
is the same mistake `check-evidence-commands.py` describes in its own docstring:

$ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast 2>&1 | tee cargo-test.log
$ echo "${PIPESTATUS[0]}"
    0

$ grep -c 'test result:' cargo-test.log
    386

$ grep -o 'test result: [a-z]*' cargo-test.log | sort | uniq -c
        386 test result: ok

$ awk '/^test result:/{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++){if($i=="passed;")p+=$(i-1);if($i=="failed;")f+=$(i-1);if($i=="ignored;")g+=$(i-1)}}END{printf "passed: %d failed: %d ignored: %d\n", p, f, g}' cargo-test.log
    passed: 31594 failed: 0 ignored: 485

The missing piece is `--env RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216` in the container runner's `run_args`.
Some of `xai-grok-shell`'s current-thread actor tests overflow the harness default stack; that
is why the container sets it at all, and `docs/architecture/todo-open-item-classification.md`
records the CI run where it was first measured. The host runner had copied the command and
left the environment behind, so a green container and a red host were both correct.

The fix mirrors the variable, and the header now prints what it mirrored so a filtered or
overridden run cannot be mistaken for a plain sweep:

    RUST_MIN_STACK_DEFAULT=16777216
    export RUST_MIN_STACK="${RUST_MIN_STACK:-${RUST_MIN_STACK_DEFAULT}}"

After it, the same leg through the runner, with the variable unset in the caller's environment
so the mirror is what does the work (871 s):

$ env -u RUST_MIN_STACK scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build --only "cargo test"
    == gates extracted from /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
       host run: the ${bootstrap} prefix is dropped, build gates are included
       env: RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 (mirrors the container's --env; set it to override)
       --only is in effect (1 pattern(s)); this is a filtered run, not a sweep
    PASS  cargo test

    all gates passed on the host (1 run, 0 skipped, 1 of 34 selected by --only)

The constant is not duplicated a second time: the self-test reads it back out of the container
runner, so the two cannot drift apart silently.

    container_stack="$(sed -n 's/.*--env RUST_MIN_STACK=\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p' \
      "${repo_root}/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh")"

== 2. --only, because a full sweep is not always the question being asked

Both runners had one mode: everything. Reproducing one defect meant either a 23-minute
container `--full` or a 25-minute host sweep, and neither could be pointed at a single gate
without an edit to a tracked file. `--only <label-substring>` filters in both, repeatable, and
a pattern that names no gate is an error rather than an empty run:

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "no such gate anywhere"
    == preparing cargo cache volumes
    == source tree: 4002 files, checksum 3813834475
       10 path(s) differ from HEAD, so this run describes the working tree, not a commit
    --only no such gate anywhere: no gate label matches it.
               labels are listed by: scripts/verify-gates.sh --list
               (cargo test and GUI protocol types are appended by --full)
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=2

The banner above the error is the container startup that had already happened; the pattern is
rejected before any gate is selected, so nothing runs.

The cost of the misreading is the reason the summary line changes. A filtered pass says so,
counts what ran against what exists, and the host runner adds a guard for the case where every
selected gate was skipped, which used to print as an all-clear over zero gates:

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "cargo fmt" --only "secret scan"
    == cargo fmt

    == secret scan
    secret-scan: clean (4001 files)

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 2 of 32 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

Measured with the filter, in the frozen image: `--only "cargo fmt"` 13 s, the two-gate run
above 11 s, `--only "cargo test"` on the host 871 s. Unfiltered, the container `--full` sweep
is 1410 s (see `verify-in-docker-full-8a52ff0a-2026-10-04.log`) and the host `--with-build`
sweep 1509 s.

`verify-in-docker.sh` deliberately has no `--list` of its own; both runners point at
`scripts/verify-gates.sh --list`, which prints all 34 labels, so there is one list to keep
correct.

== 3. The self-test grew from 24 to 47 cases, and five mutations were used to check it

The `--only` rules are behavioural, so they are pinned with generated gate lists in the
runner's own `--self-test`. The fixtures include a pair where the excluded middle gate exits
7, which is what proves the filter excludes rather than merely selects:

$ scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test
    verify-gates self-test: 47/47 cases pass

Each number below is the count of `not ok` lines reported against the mutated runner, so a
count that matches the new `--only` cases exactly means the rest of the runner is unaffected
by it:

  - filter matching removed, so the run takes every gate anyway: 10 cases fail
  - the pre-validation of `--only` patterns removed, so a typo selects nothing and passes: 2 fail
  - the "nothing ran" guard removed: 4 fail
  - `RUST_MIN_STACK_DEFAULT` changed to 8388608, so the mirrored size is wrong: 1 fails
  - the export deleted outright (first attempt): 28 fail, but that run is confounded and was redone

The last one is worth writing down. Replacing the export with `:  # MUTATION` killed the
runner at startup, before any case ran, because the header banner read `${RUST_MIN_STACK}`
under `set -u` and an unbound variable there is fatal. The mutation was confounded by the very
line added in section 1, so the banner was changed to `${RUST_MIN_STACK:-unset}` and the
mutation re-run: the environment-mirror case fails alone, 1 `not ok`, 46 pass. Every mutated
file was restored with a plain `cp` and verified with `cmp` before the final 47/47 run.

== 4. The container runner died before printing its own verdict

`--only "cargo clippy"` in the frozen image ran 164 s, printed clippy's completion, and
stopped. No `PASS`, no `FAILED gates:` line, nothing:

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "cargo clippy"
    (clippy's 400+ Checking lines elided here)
        Checking xai-grok-pager v0.4.2 (/src/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager)
        Checking xai-grok-pager-minimal v0.1.0 (/src/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager-minimal)
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 2m 40s

It reproduced with the tree at rest, which ruled out the gate. The runner compares a
fingerprint of the source tree before and after the gates, and if the tree moved it lists what
changed and declares the run unattributable. That comparison was:

    moved="$(diff "${tree_before}" "${tree_after}" | sed -n 's/^[<>] [0-9][0-9]* [0-9][0-9]* //p' | sort -u)"

`diff` exits 1 when the two files differ, which is exactly the case the branch exists to
report. In a `set -euo pipefail` script a plain assignment inherits its command substitution's
status, so the script ended inside the assignment. The four-line isolation:

$ bash -c 'set -euo pipefail
      printf "a\n" > /tmp/pf-demo/before; printf "b\n" > /tmp/pf-demo/after
      moved="$(diff /tmp/pf-demo/before /tmp/pf-demo/after | sed -n "s/^[<>] //p" | sort -u)"
      echo "reached: ${moved}"'
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=1

$ bash -c 'set -euo pipefail
      moved="$(diff /tmp/pf-demo/before /tmp/pf-demo/after | sed -n "s/^[<>] //p" | sort -u || true)"
      echo "reached: ${moved}"'
    reached: a
    b
    EXIT=0

So the failure had one author and one victim: any tracked file touched while the gates ran (an
edit in the second window, a formatter hook) turned the runner into a process that exits 1
having said nothing about why. The gate results were already lost by the time it died, because
the verdict is printed after the fingerprint comparison.

Reproduction of the whole shape, in the frozen image, editing one tracked file from a second
window while the gate runs, then putting it back:

$ bak="$(mktemp)" && cp docs/ci-test-debt.md "${bak}"
$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "timeout children"
$ sleep 14 && printf '<!-- mid-run probe -->\n' >> docs/ci-test-debt.md
$ cp "${bak}" docs/ci-test-debt.md && cmp "${bak}" docs/ci-test-debt.md && echo RESTORE-IDENTICAL
    RESTORE-IDENTICAL

Before the fix (the gate itself had passed, and the report this function exists to produce was
the thing that never ran):

    Ran 38 tests in 30.726s

    OK (skipped=2)
    timeout children hold: 12 timeout-abandoned output/status site(s), 12 kill their child on
    drop, 0 are std commands, 0 recorded
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=1

After the fix, same probe, same exit code, and this time the reader is told what happened:

    timeout children hold: 12 timeout-abandoned output/status site(s), 12 kill their child on
    drop, 0 are std commands, 0 recorded

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 1 of 32 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

    UNATTRIBUTABLE: the source tree changed while the gates ran.
      checksum 1937944216 -> 4088590097; these paths differ:
        docs/ci-test-debt.md
      nothing in this run can be attributed to a commit; re-run it with the tree at rest.
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=1

The probe file was restored with `cp` and verified with `cmp` (RESTORE-IDENTICAL) after both
runs.

== 5. What was not done here, and why

`verify-in-docker.sh` has no automated fixture for this verdict path. There is no seam: the
fingerprint is taken inside `main` around the gate loop, and the honest reproduction is the
`--only "cargo fmt"` plus mid-run tracked-file edit shown above, which costs 13 s plus a
probe. The host runner's equivalent logic does have fixtures, in `--self-test`.

The same assignment shape is written elsewhere in the tracked shell scripts too. The scan, the
list of what is left, and why each remaining site is correct to stop rather than report, are
in `shell-pipefail-silent-report-2026-10-04.log`. In short: there the substitution's output is
the thing being tested, and it fails only on a genuine error (a `sha256sum` over a tunnel that
is not up, a host that cannot answer `uname`), where stopping is the right answer and there is
no report to lose.

== 6. Reproduce it

$ scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test
$ scripts/verify-gates.sh --list
$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "cargo fmt"
$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "no such gate anywhere"
$ scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build --only "cargo test"

The last one is 15 minutes and needs the workspace build cache; it is the check that the
mirrored stack size is the one `xai-grok-shell` needs.
